  <title>
    <media type="image" width="40px" height="40px" its:translate="no" src="figures/orca-logo.svg" /> Backing up, restoring, and resetting settings
  </title>
  <section id="backing-up-settings">
    <title>Backing up settings</title>
    <p>
      Orca stores its settings in dconf under <code>/org/gnome/orca/</code>. You can use the
      following terminal commands to keep a backup or copy your settings to another machine,
      including profiles and application-specific settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Save any changes in Orca's Preferences window before making a backup. Run <code>dconf dump /org/gnome/orca/ &gt; orca-settings.ini</code>
      to save your settings to a file named <code>orca-settings.ini</code> in the terminal's
      current directory. Choose a different filename to keep an earlier backup.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="restoring-or-transferring-settings">
    <title>Restoring or transferring settings</title>
    <p>
      To transfer settings to another machine, copy the backup file there. The voices and
      braille support your settings use must also be available on that machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Close Orca's Preferences window before restoring settings. From the directory containing
      your backup, run <code>dconf load /org/gnome/orca/ &lt; orca-settings.ini</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loading a backup overwrites the settings present in the file but leaves other existing
      settings unchanged. To replace your settings with the backup instead, first back up any
      current settings you want to keep, then run <code>dconf reset -f /org/gnome/orca/ &amp;&amp; dconf load /org/gnome/orca/ &lt; orca-settings.ini</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Restart Orca after restoring settings so all changes take effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      See also:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <link xref="introduction">Starting and stopping Orca</link>: Start and stop
          Orca using your desktop or the command line.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="resetting-all-settings">
    <title>Resetting all settings</title>
    <p>
      To start fresh, close Orca's Preferences window and run <code>dconf reset -f /org/gnome/orca/</code>. This removes all
      your saved Orca settings, including profiles, application-specific settings, voice
      choices, and command bindings. Make a backup first if you might want to restore them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Restart Orca to use the default settings.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="backing-up-restoring-or-resetting-one-profile">
    <title>Backing up, restoring, or resetting one profile</title>
    <p>
      You can also back up, restore, or reset settings for a single profile. These examples use
      the default profile:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          Back up: <code>dconf dump /org/gnome/orca/default/ &gt; default-profile.ini</code>
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          Restore: <code>dconf load /org/gnome/orca/default/ &lt; default-profile.ini</code>
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          Reset: <code>dconf reset -f /org/gnome/orca/default/</code>
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
    <p>
      To back up, restore, or reset another profile, replace <code>default</code> in the path with its stored
      identifier. Run <code>dconf list /org/gnome/orca/</code> to list the stored
      profile directories; their names can differ from the names shown in preferences.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same backup and restore precautions apply to individual profiles. Resetting a
      non-default profile also removes it from Orca's profile list; recreate it in preferences
      if you want to use it again. Changes to the default profile can also affect other profiles
      that inherit its settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      See also:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <link xref="features_profiles">Profiles</link>: Create collections
          of settings for different activities.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
